  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Jung Hoo Lee hit a solo shot and two RBIs, but ten strikeouts and two total hits meant the Giants never had enough. Down 2\u20130 after the first, they chased all night and couldn't close the gap. Roupp was solid through 5.1 innings. He gave up four runs on six hits with seven strikeouts. And the bullpen kept it from getting worse. That wasn't the problem. The problem was the lineup didn't threaten after scoring twice in the fifth. This is a team that needs runners to be moving to survive in the division.",
    "what_this_means": "A one-run game through five innings turned into a two-run deficit by the ninth. The Dodgers pushed through with three in the sixth while the Giants couldn't answer. Lee's power was real, but power without traffic doesn't change ballgames. The offense generated almost nothing besides one moment from one player. They had chances in the middle innings and left them empty. Ten strikeouts and a .240 team average is not a way to stay eight games back and hold on. The margin is tightening the other way.",
    "what_to_watch": "Aaron Civale takes the mound for Oakland tonight at 6:40 PT with a 2.59 ERA. Our pitching staff needs to match that control, because this lineup cannot afford another night of chasing.",
